1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,560
 In the previous few lectures, we looked at our decision point one, how we can select the right

2
00:00:04,560 --> 00:00:06,640
 AWD service.

3
00:00:06,640 --> 00:00:11,040
 Now we come to the second decision point, how do we select the right foundation model for

4
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:12,040
 our use case.

5
00:00:12,040 --> 00:00:17,240
 Now in the next few lectures, we are going to focus on how do we select the right foundation

6
00:00:17,240 --> 00:00:18,240
 model.

7
00:00:18,240 --> 00:00:23,640
 Now let's start with the factors that impact the choice of our foundation models.

8
00:00:23,640 --> 00:00:29,320
 So the first thing we need to think about is the modality of our foundation model.

9
00:00:29,320 --> 00:00:34,040
 So let's say your use case is focused more on text, like text summarization, content

10
00:00:34,040 --> 00:00:38,840
 generation, then your foundation model should have modality as text.

11
00:00:38,840 --> 00:00:43,280
 Now let's say your use case is focused on advertisement and as part of that, you need to

12
00:00:43,280 --> 00:00:46,480
 create images, videos and so on.

13
00:00:46,480 --> 00:00:51,080
 Then the modality of the foundation model you select should be image.

14
00:00:51,080 --> 00:00:55,560
 Now there are certain scenarios like in our use case for manufacturing where we are going

15
00:00:55,560 --> 00:01:00,120
 to send the images as well as text to our foundation model.

16
00:01:00,120 --> 00:01:05,840
 Then it should support multimodal where it supports both text and image processing.

17
00:01:05,840 --> 00:01:10,720
 And that's sometimes called as text vision in Amazon better terms.

18
00:01:10,720 --> 00:01:12,080
 And then finally we have the embedding.

19
00:01:12,080 --> 00:01:16,560
 So there are some foundation models that support the embedding modality.

20
00:01:16,560 --> 00:01:21,560
 So what they do is let's say we have a text document or image, they would create a vector

21
00:01:21,560 --> 00:01:27,240
 representation of that image or dot of the document or image and then store.

22
00:01:27,240 --> 00:01:30,760
 So it's mainly used for search and retrieval process.

23
00:01:30,760 --> 00:01:36,040
 So based on your use case, you need to select the foundation model with right modality.

24
00:01:36,040 --> 00:01:39,200
 Next you should assess the task complexity of your use case.

25
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:43,720
 So whether it's a use case like summarization, which is much easier or there's a lot

26
00:01:43,720 --> 00:01:48,920
 of reasoning involved in your use case to process the user request.

27
00:01:48,920 --> 00:01:52,960
 And that to a large extent will decide what kind of foundation model will you use whether

28
00:01:52,960 --> 00:01:57,240
 you will use a small language model or a large language model.

29
00:01:57,240 --> 00:02:02,480
 So usually small language models are classified which have parameters up to 8 billion and large

30
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:06,640
 language models could have hundreds of billions of parameters.

31
00:02:06,640 --> 00:02:07,840
 So next is the context window.

32
00:02:07,840 --> 00:02:14,480
 So context window means what is the maximum amount of words that you can input at one point

33
00:02:14,480 --> 00:02:17,960
 of time to your foundation model.

34
00:02:17,960 --> 00:02:23,720
 So it varies between model to model and it can range anything from 8K input size token

35
00:02:23,720 --> 00:02:25,040
 to 200K.

36
00:02:25,040 --> 00:02:27,640
 So Cloud and Tropic supports 200K token.

37
00:02:27,640 --> 00:02:30,360
 We know 1000 tokens is approximately 750 words.

38
00:02:30,360 --> 00:02:38,680
 So 200K maximal token size would be about 150K words which you can send as input.

39
00:02:38,680 --> 00:02:42,800
 Now there could be some scenarios, let's say you are generating content.

40
00:02:42,800 --> 00:02:46,760
 So your input token size might be much smaller.

41
00:02:46,760 --> 00:02:50,920
 But there could also be scenarios, let's say we are working in the legal department of

42
00:02:50,920 --> 00:02:56,960
 an organization and you are and you want to summarize your contract document.

43
00:02:56,960 --> 00:03:01,200
 So in that case the contract document size could be in hundreds of pages.

44
00:03:01,200 --> 00:03:05,120
 In that scenario you might need a very, very large context window.

45
00:03:05,120 --> 00:03:10,200
 So that's why it's important to understand what is the context window size for a large

46
00:03:10,200 --> 00:03:12,240
 language model.

47
00:03:12,240 --> 00:03:17,800
 Okay, next one is the guard rail and safety in most foundation models.

48
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:22,720
 There are some built in guard rail and additional capability around toxicity, content moderation.

49
00:03:22,720 --> 00:03:26,120
 So let's say you have built a generic application using the foundation model.

50
00:03:26,120 --> 00:03:32,520
 And now one of the end users pose a question like I want to know how I can kill someone.

51
00:03:32,520 --> 00:03:36,760
 So you don't want your foundation model to answer those sort of questions.

52
00:03:36,760 --> 00:03:40,400
 So most foundation owners have already have some form of guard rail.

53
00:03:40,400 --> 00:03:45,200
 So you might want to analyze what kind of guard rail are available in built as part of

54
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:46,360
 the large language model.

55
00:03:46,360 --> 00:03:49,880
 Though you can supplement it based on your organization guidelines as well through better

56
00:03:49,880 --> 00:03:55,440
 guard rails and SageMaker custom guard rail, but you should also evaluate what are the

57
00:03:55,440 --> 00:03:58,960
 kind of guard rail available to the large language model.

58
00:03:58,960 --> 00:04:02,840
 Next we have the quality and accuracy of the output.

59
00:04:02,840 --> 00:04:09,040
 So you can use the better console to evaluate foundation models from different model providers

60
00:04:09,040 --> 00:04:14,560
 and see what is the kind of output it is generating for you.

61
00:04:14,560 --> 00:04:19,880
 You can then you can also use the bedrock model output evaluation tool to automatically

62
00:04:19,880 --> 00:04:26,800
 or through humans evaluate the output of different foundation model and see if the kind

63
00:04:26,800 --> 00:04:32,160
 of response that you are getting fits based on your use case.

64
00:04:32,160 --> 00:04:35,560
 Then you have cost predictability and token efficiency.

65
00:04:35,560 --> 00:04:42,000
 You can evaluate the cost per thousand tokens for different model providers as well

66
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:45,760
 as different models from the same model provider.

67
00:04:45,760 --> 00:04:49,400
 And this is important because let's say you are doing a pucie, so let's say you are sending

68
00:04:49,400 --> 00:04:54,120
 a few 100 requests and and in bedrock each request could cost a few cents.

69
00:04:54,120 --> 00:04:55,760
 So it might not be a lot of cost.

70
00:04:55,760 --> 00:05:00,200
 But when you do that at a scale at a production grade scale where you are sending hundreds

71
00:05:00,200 --> 00:05:06,320
 and thousands of requests over a period of year that there could be a enormous cost difference.

72
00:05:06,320 --> 00:05:12,400
 So that's why you have to optimize your foundation model based on the cost per thousand

73
00:05:12,400 --> 00:05:13,400
 token.

74
00:05:13,400 --> 00:05:18,080
 Then you also need to look at regional availability and quotas.

75
00:05:18,080 --> 00:05:22,520
 So lot of foundation models are not available in many different regions.

76
00:05:22,520 --> 00:05:27,800
 You should evaluate whether that foundation model is available within your region.

77
00:05:27,800 --> 00:05:33,400
 And then bedrock and also set some quotas for the foundation models on number of requests

78
00:05:33,400 --> 00:05:37,160
 that you can make per minute in that region.

79
00:05:37,160 --> 00:05:43,240
 So you should look at quotas and it meets the throughput requirements of your journey application

80
00:05:43,240 --> 00:05:47,200
 using that foundation model.

81
00:05:47,200 --> 00:05:50,360
 Next and something that is really really important is latency.

82
00:05:50,360 --> 00:05:54,880
 So latency is really important from the end user experience perspective.

83
00:05:54,880 --> 00:06:00,080
 Think about using chat GPT and you pose a question and gives your response in three minutes.

84
00:06:00,080 --> 00:06:03,160
 So that user experience would not be great.

85
00:06:03,160 --> 00:06:08,160
 So that's why from the end user experience latency optimization is critical.

86
00:06:08,160 --> 00:06:13,080
 And there are a lot of foundation models which bedrock provides which are latency optimized

87
00:06:13,080 --> 00:06:18,080
 inference model and you can evaluate whether you actually need those sort of models.

88
00:06:18,080 --> 00:06:23,960
 And we're going to discuss latency optimize inference models later in the course.

89
00:06:23,960 --> 00:06:26,920
 And then we have the proprietary versus open source.

90
00:06:26,920 --> 00:06:32,760
 So you should always perform open source models compared to proprietary ones.

91
00:06:32,760 --> 00:06:35,200
 So then finally we have the language support.

92
00:06:35,200 --> 00:06:38,400
 So not all foundation models support all the languages.

93
00:06:38,400 --> 00:06:43,840
 So you should go ahead and evaluate what are the languages supported by that particular

94
00:06:43,840 --> 00:06:47,640
 foundation model based on your use case.

95
00:06:47,640 --> 00:06:54,480
 And finally in summary select the smallest safest and most cost efficient model that meets

96
00:06:54,480 --> 00:07:00,040
 your accuracy latency and governess requirements for your workload.

97
00:07:00,040 --> 00:07:01,040
 Okay.

98
00:07:01,040 --> 00:07:04,960
 So those are some of the factors you should think about when you are selecting the foundation

99
00:07:04,960 --> 00:07:07,560
 model for your use case.

100
00:07:07,560 --> 00:07:08,560
 That's pretty much from this lecture.

101
00:07:08,560 --> 00:07:09,560
 I'll see you in the next lecture.

102
00:07:09,560 --> 00:07:09,880
 Thank you.

